  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nToday-Monday: Heat Returns This Weekend Into Early Next Week, Storm \nChances\n\nAfter one last nice day, the upper level ridge becomes more \npronounced and builds northward. Before this ridge builds in, there \nremains southeasterly winds in the lowest levels, which will help to \nkeep temperatures in the mid 70s to low 80s for today. Heading into \nSaturday the upper level ridge begins to build northward. This ridge \nwill be at its strongest on Sunday with 850mb mean temperatures, \nbased on the LREF, of 23 to 27C across the CWA. Southwesterly flow \nalso returns in the low levels which in turn will increase dewpoints \nthrough early next week. If we include the corn sweat, dewpoints are \nexpected to be in the 70s, with some locations in northeast Iowa and \nsoutheast Minnesota potentially seeing 80+ degree dewpoints. When \ncombining these dewpoints with the heat in place, heat indices are \nexpected to be in the 100 to 110F range. These hot temperatures \ncreep into our CWA on Saturday and we have issued a Heat Advisory \nfor portions of southeast Minnesota. As a result of the hot \ntemperatures for Sunday, we have issued an Extreme Heat Watch for \nmuch of the forecast area for Sunday and again for portions of \nnortheast Iowa and far southwest Wisconsin for Monday. Conditions \nslightly improve on Monday as ensembles favor a slight southern \nshift in the ridge, with the hottest conditions in northeast Iowa, \nsouthwest Wisconsin, and the Mississippi River Valley with heat \nindices in the 100 to 110F range. One thing to note is that there is \na little uncertainty on meeting heat advisory or extreme heat \nwarning criteria due to potential convection and smoke from Canadian \nwildfires. While the smoke is not expected to be a problem near the \nsurface, it will be aloft and depending on the concentration of \nsmoke, this could have an impact on temperatures by lowering them a \nbit.\n\nFocusing on the storm chances, today remains dry as we have that \nlight southeast flow near the surface. A couple of weak shortwaves \nmove through on Saturday allowing for increased rain chances for \nwestern Wisconsin for Saturday morning and again for portions of \nnorth central Wisconsin for Saturday evening. With the forecast area \nremaining in northwest flow aloft, any shortwave that follows the \njet stream will potentially impact the forecast area. The first \ninstance is on Sunday evening when a potential MCS moves through. \nGuidance continues to favor southeast Minnesota and northeast Iowa \nas the locations that this could impact, however with any MCS, the \nlocation will remain uncertain until the day of. Throughout the day \non Sunday, there will be plenty of instability (2500 to 3500 J/kg of \nMLCAPE) and deep layer shear (40 to 45 kts of 0-6km shear) for \nstorms, however the main uncertainty for storm development is \ncapping as temperatures near 850mb will be around 23 to 27C. More \nthunderstorms are possible Monday morning, however the severe storm \nthreat should decrease heading into morning hours.\n\nTuesday-Friday: Warm Temperatures Continue, Storm Chances Continue\n\nEnsemble guidance continues to suggest that this ridge will shift \nslightly westward keeping the Upper Midwest in northwest flow aloft. \nThis will allow for waves to ride the top of the ridge and \npotentially impact the region giving us more rain chances. \nTemperatures look to be slightly cooler than Sunday and Monday due \nto the ridge shifting a little further west. Highs are currently in \nthe mid 80s to near 90F for most locations through the end of the \nweek.",
